the Continental Congress had had a  president but the president of the  Continental Congress was an office which  involved little more than serving as the  chairman of the Congress's sessions  several of the state constitutions had  tinkered with the notion of establishing  an executive committee rather than  vesting power in a single individual  like a governor and whatever form a  national executive might take everybody  wanted to avoid going to the other  extreme and resurrecting the idea of a  monarch for America now these concerns  make the executive designed by the  convention all the more surprising  because the new constitution would vest  the entire executive power in a single  person a president who would have  general responsibilities for seeing that  congressional legislation was duly  executed who would be commander in chief  of the Army and Navy and time of war and  who would be the general supervisor of  the Republic's Foreign Relations nothing  like the old chairman of proceedings in  the Confederation Congress furthermore  by passing the jealous interests of the  states the president was to be elected  by the people of the United States as a  whole now this presidency struck many of  the old revolutionaries as the most  objectionable feature of the new  constitution Patrick Henry protested  that the tyranny of Philadelphia may be  like the tyranny of George the third and  that might have been the fishbone that  the states choked on were not for two  concessions that were made first of all  the convention amended the process  whereby the president was elected so  that Americans would not vote directly  for the president and thus put the  states on the shelf but they would vote  indirectly Americans would vote in their  states for state electors who were  supposed to represent the various  candidates for president  these electors would then assemble as a  college not a college as an educational  institution a college in the older sense  of the word which simply means an  assembly the electors would come  together as an electoral college and  then they would cast votes for whichever  candidate they represented whichever  candidate tallied up the most votes in  the electoral college was to be  President in fact that this meant that  while the people voted for the president  it was the states through the state  electors who elected him the other  concession which made the new presidency  more palatable was the unspoken  assumption that George Washington a  figure of universal Trust would be the  president under the new constitution in  fact one thing which made the  Philadelphia Convention as successful as  it was was the fact that Washington was  invited and agreed to become the  chairman of the convention the prospect  of this same man the hero of the  Continental Army  now the successful presiding officer of  the convention the idea that this  Washington would also be as everyone  assumed the first president under the  new constitution did a great deal to  disarm objections to the notion of the  presidency as outlined in the new  document
 

